('127.98.d.18.A',4,'A tool used to ensure a post is vertical is a:','["Chalk eraser", "Calculator only", "Ruler only", "Plumb bob or level"]'::jsonb,3,'Plumb checks vertical alignment.','original'),
('127.98.d.18.B',3,'Wood preservatives, adhesives, and solvents used by carpenters are examples of:','["Environmentally hazardous materials", "Water", "Food", "Soil"]'::jsonb,0,'They require careful disposal.','original'),
('127.98.d.18.B',4,'Empty adhesive tubes with residue should be:','["Buried", "Disposed of following local hazardous waste rules", "Thrown in a stream", "Burned"]'::jsonb,1,'Disposal rules protect the environment.','original'),
('127.98.d.18.C',3,'Scaffold platforms must be:','["Partly open", "Placed on loose bricks", "Fully planked and supported on a stable base", "Left unsecured"]'::jsonb,2,'Stable scaffolds prevent falls.','original'),
('127.98.d.18.C',4,'Guardrails on scaffolds are required to prevent:','["Sunburn", "Noise", "Dust", "Falls from height"]'::jsonb,3,'Edge protection saves lives.','original'),
('127.98.d.18.D',3,'An emerging carpentry technology is:','["Laser measuring and cutting guides", "Quill pens", "Stone hammers", "Flint tools"]'::jsonb,0,'Digital tools improve accuracy.','original'),
('127.98.d.18.D',4,'Building information modeling (BIM) helps carpenters by:','["Cutting wood", "Showing a 3D model of the project", "Driving trucks", "Making coffee"]'::jsonb,1,'BIM helps coordination.','original'),
('127.98.d.19.A',3,'To cut a board to 36 inches, a carpenter should first:','["Cut randomly", "Guess", "Measure, mark, and square the line", "Skip marking"]'::jsonb,2,'Accuracy begins with marking.','original'),
('127.98.d.19.A',4,'A crisp pencil line is better than a thick crayon mark because it:','["Wastes wood", "Costs more", "Is harder to see", "Gives a more accurate cut line"]'::jsonb,3,'Fine lines reduce errors.','original'),
('127.98.d.19.B',3,'A beam carries a load that pushes straight down. This is called a:','["Gravity load", "Zero load", "Side load", "Wind load"]'::jsonb,0,'Gravity loads act vertically.','original'),
('127.98.d.19.B',4,'Wind pushing sideways on a wall is called a:','["Stationary load", "Lateral load", "Dead load", "Vertical load"]'::jsonb,1,'Lateral loads act horizontally.','original'),
('127.98.d.19.C',3,'Rafters are framing members used to build a:','["Floor", "Foundation", "Roof", "Driveway"]'::jsonb,2,'Rafters support roof sheathing.','original'),
('127.98.d.19.C',4,'Floor joists are typically spaced:','["1 inch apart", "8 feet apart", "Randomly", "16 inches on center"]'::jsonb,3,'16 inches on center is common.','original'),
('127.98.d.19.D',3,'Drywall screws should be spaced:','["According to the fastener schedule", "Only at corners", "Randomly", "As far apart as possible"]'::jsonb,0,'Schedules ensure holding power.','original'),
('127.98.d.19.D',4,'Drywall sheets are usually installed with the long edge:','["Diagonally", "Perpendicular to the framing", "Vertically only", "Parallel to the framing"]'::jsonb,1,'Perpendicular placement strengthens joints.','original'),
('127.98.d.19.E',3,'Before setting a window, the opening is checked for:','["Bright color", "Noise", "Plumb, level, and square", "Tight wallpaper"]'::jsonb,2,'A true opening ensures fit.','original'),
('127.98.d.19.E',4,'Trim installed around a door is called:','["Siding", "Shingle", "Footing", "Casing"]'::jsonb,3,'Casing covers the gap around a door.','original'),
('127.98.d.20.A',3,'A framing hammer is used primarily to:','["Drive nails", "Cut boards", "Measure", "Level floors"]'::jsonb,0,'Framing hammers drive large nails.','original'),
('127.98.d.20.A',4,'A tool with a bubble vial for checking whether a surface is horizontal or vertical is a:','["Mallet", "Level", "Stapler", "Chisel"]'::jsonb,1,'Levels check horizontal and vertical.','original'),
('127.98.d.20.B',3,'When using a chisel, keep both hands:','["In front of the edge", "On the blade", "Behind the cutting edge", "Holding the work against your body"]'::jsonb,2,'Hands stay clear of the cutting path.','original'),
('127.98.d.20.B',4,'Clamping a workpiece when using a hand saw makes the cut:','["More dangerous", "Slower only", "Less accurate", "Safer and more accurate"]'::jsonb,3,'Secure work prevents slipping.','original'),
('127.98.d.20.C',3,'A general rule for all power tools is to wear:','["Eye protection", "Open shirts", "Sandals", "Jewelry"]'::jsonb,0,'Eye protection is universal.','original'),
('127.98.d.20.C',4,'Before changing a bit or blade, a power tool should be:','["Left plugged in", "Unplugged or its battery removed", "Running", "Set on high"]'::jsonb,1,'Disconnect power for safety.','original'),
('127.98.d.20.D',3,'A portable tool that spins a round blade to cut lumber is the:','["Coping saw", "Block plane", "Circular saw", "Mallet"]'::jsonb,2,'Circular saws are portable.','original'),
('127.98.d.20.D',4,'A tool used to smooth surfaces by abrasion is a:','["Plumb bob", "Level", "Stud finder", "Sander"]'::jsonb,3,'Sanders smooth wood.','original'),
('127.98.d.20.E',3,'When using a portable saw, support the workpiece so the cut-off piece:','["Does not pinch the blade", "Binds", "Moves freely", "Falls on your foot"]'::jsonb,0,'Pinching causes kickback.','original'),
('127.98.d.20.E',4,'Letting a saw reach full speed before cutting helps to:','["Burn the wood", "Avoid binding and kickback", "Waste battery", "Dull the blade"]'::jsonb,1,'Full speed gives a cleaner entry.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.A',3,'A set of construction plans commonly contains:','["Recipes", "Poems", "Site, floor, framing, and elevation drawings", "Menus"]'::jsonb,2,'Plans have multiple sheet types.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.A',4,'The sheet that shows the building''s footprint on the lot is the:','["Wall section", "Door schedule", "Roof detail", "Site plan"]'::jsonb,3,'Site plans show placement.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.B',3,'On a plan, a dashed line typically shows:','["Hidden features", "Center of the sun", "Visible edges", "Dimensions"]'::jsonb,0,'Dashes mean hidden.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.B',4,'A thin line with arrows at each end is a:','["Hidden line", "Dimension line", "Wall edge", "Section cut"]'::jsonb,1,'Dimension lines mark measurements.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.C',3,'On a section drawing, a pattern of closely spaced diagonal lines often represents:','["The sky", "Grass", "A cut material", "Water"]'::jsonb,2,'Hatching shows cut surfaces.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.C',4,'Insulation in a wall section is often shown as:','["A solid black bar", "A dotted circle", "A smooth blank", "A wavy or zigzag pattern"]'::jsonb,3,'Convention shows batts.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.D',3,'A symbol for a floor drain would appear on the:','["Plumbing plan", "Landscape sketch", "Site survey only", "Roof framing plan"]'::jsonb,0,'Drains are plumbing items.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.D',4,'A thermostat symbol appears on:','["Landscape plans", "Mechanical or electrical drawings", "Site drawings only", "Window schedules only"]'::jsonb,1,'Controls are shown on systems sheets.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.E',3,'The plan abbreviation ''WH'' might stand for:','["Wood house", "Wide hallway", "Water heater", "Wall hook"]'::jsonb,2,'Check the abbreviation list.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.E',4,'The abbreviation ''O.C.'' on a framing plan means:','["Open closet", "Out of code", "Over ceiling", "On center"]'::jsonb,3,'It describes spacing.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.F',3,'To find the thickness of a wall, a carpenter would study a:','["Wall section", "Menu", "Weather report", "Parking plan"]'::jsonb,0,'Sections show assemblies.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.F',4,'A window schedule gives each window''s:','["Crew member''s names", "Size, type, and location code", "Owner''s pets", "Price at lunch"]'::jsonb,1,'Schedules list data.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.G',3,'The purpose of written specifications is to:','["Sell tools", "Bake cookies", "Define the quality of materials and workmanship", "Replace plans"]'::jsonb,2,'Specs add detail drawings can''t show.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.G',4,'If a specification calls for a certain product by name, the contractor must:','["Pick any product", "Delay the job", "Ignore it", "Use it or get approval for a substitute"]'::jsonb,3,'Specs are part of the contract.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.H',3,'A quantity takeoff for a 12-foot by 10-foot deck floor needs how many square feet of decking?','["120", "22", "240", "44"]'::jsonb,0,'12 x 10 = 120.','original'),
('127.98.d.21.H',4,'After figuring the decking area, adding about 10 percent more material allows for:','["Tips", "Waste from cuts", "Weight", "Rain"]'::jsonb,1,'Waste factor is standard.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.A',3,'Platform framing is a system in which each floor:','["Floats", "Has no joists", "Is built as a platform on top of the walls below", "Is built in the foundation only"]'::jsonb,2,'Platform framing is most common.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.A',4,'A framing system where wall studs run from foundation to roof is called:','["Pole barn", "Platform framing", "Timber peg", "Balloon framing"]'::jsonb,3,'Balloon framing uses long studs.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.B',3,'A floor system''s drawings and specs show joist size and:','["Spacing", "Age", "Brand of paint", "Color"]'::jsonb,0,'Spacing affects strength.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.B',4,'To determine the required joist size, a carpenter refers to the:','["Phone list", "Span tables or engineered plans", "Weather map", "Calendar"]'::jsonb,1,'Span tables give allowed sizes.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.C',3,'The member that sits on the foundation and supports floor joists is the:','["Ridge", "Rafter", "Sill plate", "Soffit"]'::jsonb,2,'Sills anchor the floor system.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.C',4,'The framing member that closes the ends of floor joists is the:','["Ridge board", "Header", "Purlin", "Rim joist (band joist)"]'::jsonb,3,'Rim joists cap the ends.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.D',3,'Sills are fastened to the foundation with:','["Anchor bolts", "Rope", "Tape", "Wood glue only"]'::jsonb,0,'Bolts prevent uplift.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.D',4,'A sill sealer is placed under the sill plate to:','["Hide nails", "Block air and moisture", "Add color", "Add weight"]'::jsonb,1,'Sealing improves performance.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.E',3,'A girder in a floor system:','["Is a type of siding", "Covers the roof", "Supports the joists along a main line", "Hangs the door"]'::jsonb,2,'Girders carry joist loads.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.E',4,'A longer girder span usually calls for a member that is:','["Identical", "Smaller", "Thinner", "Larger"]'::jsonb,3,'Longer spans need stronger members.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.F',3,'Bridging between joists helps to:','["Stiffen the floor and share loads", "Lower cost only", "Add noise", "Weaken the floor"]'::jsonb,0,'Bridging resists twisting.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'Career Development' and d.language = 'en'
on conflict (fingerprint) do nothing;
insert into public.question_bank (teks_code, teks_item_id, subject, grade_num, course, difficulty, qtype, prompt, choices, answer_idx, explanation, source, status, gen_batch)
select distinct on (v.prompt) v.code, i.id, d.subject,
       case when i.grade_level = 'K' then 0 when i.grade_level ~ '^[0-9]+' then substring(i.grade_level from '^[0-9]+')::smallint end,
       case when i.grade_level is null then i.grade_course_title end,
       v.diff, 'mc', v.prompt, v.choices, v.ans, v.expl, v.src, 'checked', 'cte_14_v1'
from (values
('127.98.d.22.F',4,'X-bridging is made of two pieces that:','["Run parallel", "Cross diagonally between adjacent joists", "Hang vertically", "Lie flat"]'::jsonb,1,'X patterns brace joists.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.G',3,'Common finish flooring materials include:','["Mud and straw", "Fishing line", "Hardwood, tile, and carpet", "Tissue paper"]'::jsonb,2,'Finishes vary by use.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.G',4,'Laminate flooring is often installed as a:','["Glued-down hardwood", "Tile mosaic", "Nailed slab", "Floating floor"]'::jsonb,3,'Floating floors snap together.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.H',3,'Subflooring is the layer that:','["Is nailed to the joists and supports finish flooring", "Is siding", "Covers shingles", "Hangs from ceilings"]'::jsonb,0,'Subfloors give a working surface.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.H',4,'Underlayment is installed to:','["Hold nails", "Provide a smooth base for finish flooring", "Cover pipes", "Make noise"]'::jsonb,1,'It smooths and cushions.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.I',3,'Subfloor panels are commonly fastened with:','["Tape", "Paper clips", "Ring-shank nails or screws and adhesive", "Staples only"]'::jsonb,2,'Ringshanks resist withdrawal.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.I',4,'Using fasteners that are too short in a subfloor can cause:','["Warm floors", "Stronger floors", "Quiet floors", "Loose panels and squeaks"]'::jsonb,3,'Fastener length matters.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.J',3,'A floor is 16 feet long and joists are 16 inches on center. About how many joists are needed (add one)?','["13", "9", "16", "25"]'::jsonb,0,'192 / 16 = 12 spaces; 13 joists.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.J',4,'Four-by-eight panels cover 32 square feet. A 12 by 16 floor needs how many panels (no waste)?','["8", "6", "4", "12"]'::jsonb,1,'192 / 32 = 6.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.K',3,'When constructing a floor assembly, the first member laid out is typically the:','["Window", "Finish floor", "Sill plate", "Door"]'::jsonb,2,'Start at the bottom.','original'),
('127.98.d.22.K',4,'Check a completed floor frame for squareness by comparing:','["Weather", "Board brands", "Paint colors", "The two diagonals"]'::jsonb,3,'Equal diagonals mean square.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.A',3,'A wall layout shows the location of studs, openings, and:','["Plates and corners", "Light bulbs only", "Menus", "Paint"]'::jsonb,0,'Layout marks all framing.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.A',4,'Ceiling layout marks locate the position of:','["Furniture", "Joists and fixtures", "Appliances", "Doorknobs"]'::jsonb,1,'Ceiling joists need layout.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.B',3,'A wood-frame wall is laid out on the:','["Footings", "Roof", "Top and bottom plates", "Rafters"]'::jsonb,2,'Plates carry the marks.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.B',4,'A king stud and trimmer stud are used at a window opening to:','["Hide wires", "Hold paint", "Cover siding", "Support the header"]'::jsonb,3,'Trimmers carry the header.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.C',3,'Before raising an assembled exterior wall, the carpenter should have:','["Bracing ready to hold it plumb", "Paint", "Nothing", "A ladder only"]'::jsonb,0,'Braces hold walls upright.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.C',4,'The exterior wall assembly is nailed together while it lies:','["In the air", "Flat on the subfloor", "On the roof", "In a truck"]'::jsonb,1,'Walls are framed flat then raised.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.D',3,'Wall sheathing is installed to:','["Cool air", "Provide light", "Add rigidity and a base for siding", "Hold paint"]'::jsonb,2,'Sheathing stiffens the wall.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.D',4,'Common sheathing materials include:','["Carpet", "Cardboard", "Foam peanuts", "Plywood and OSB"]'::jsonb,3,'Panels are most common.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.E',3,'After tilting an exterior wall into place, it is temporarily held with:','["Diagonal braces", "Tape", "Paper", "Loose nails only"]'::jsonb,0,'Braces hold plumb until sheathed.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.E',4,'Holding a long level against an erected wall confirms the wall is vertical, or:','["Painted", "Plumb", "Insulated", "Dry"]'::jsonb,1,'Verticality is required.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.F',3,'When wood framing is used inside masonry walls, it is called:','["Shingling", "Pouring", "Furring", "Soffiting"]'::jsonb,2,'Furring strips attach finishes.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.F',4,'Wood framing that sits against concrete or masonry should be:','["Left bare", "Painted only", "Untreated", "Pressure-treated or protected from moisture"]'::jsonb,3,'Moisture rots untreated wood.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.G',3,'Metal studs are often used in commercial buildings because they are:','["Non-combustible and uniform", "Flammable", "Irregular", "Rot prone"]'::jsonb,0,'Steel resists fire and rot.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.G',4,'Metal studs are fastened to track with:','["Staples", "Screws", "Wood glue", "Tape"]'::jsonb,1,'Screws or crimps connect steel.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.H',3,'Ceiling joists tie the walls together and support:','["The siding", "The foundation", "Ceiling finish and, often, insulation", "The sidewalk"]'::jsonb,2,'They carry ceilings and ties.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.H',4,'When cutting ceiling joists, they should be notched only:','["Through the middle", "Anywhere", "Randomly", "According to code limits"]'::jsonb,3,'Over-notching weakens joists.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.I',3,'Studs at 16 inches on center on a 24-foot wall need about how many studs (add one)?','["19", "12", "30", "24"]'::jsonb,0,'288 / 16 = 18; plus 1 = 19.','original'),
('127.98.d.23.I',4,'Estimating framing materials should include extra for:','["Furniture", "Corners, openings, and waste", "Nothing", "Paint"]'::jsonb,1,'Extras add studs.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.A',3,'The ''run'' of a roof is the:','["Vertical height", "Slope length", "Horizontal distance from the wall to the center line", "Overhang width"]'::jsonb,2,'Run is horizontal.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.A',4,'A roof that rises 6 inches for every 12 inches of run has a pitch of:','["3 in 12", "12 in 6", "1 in 6", "6 in 12"]'::jsonb,3,'Pitch is rise over run.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.B',3,'A gable roof has two sloping planes that meet at the:','["Ridge", "Sill", "Header", "Footing"]'::jsonb,0,'The ridge is the peak.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.B',4,'A hip roof differs from a gable roof because it:','["Has no rafters", "Slopes on all four sides", "Is flat", "Has only a ridge"]'::jsonb,1,'Hip roofs slope to each wall.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.C',3,'Rafter length can be found with a:','["Clamp", "Mallet", "Framing square rafter table or the Pythagorean theorem", "Compass"]'::jsonb,2,'Both methods work.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.C',4,'A roof has 12 feet of run and 5 feet of rise. The rafter length (before overhang) is about:','["7 feet", "15 feet", "17 feet", "13 feet"]'::jsonb,3,'sqrt(144+25)=13.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.D',3,'Trusses are prefabricated frames that:','["Span the width of the building and support the roof", "Replace windows", "Hold the siding", "Cover floors"]'::jsonb,0,'Trusses are engineered.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.D',4,'A common type of truss has webs forming:','["Circles", "Triangles", "Spirals", "Squares"]'::jsonb,1,'Triangles are rigid.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.E',3,'A speed square can be used to mark:','["Pipe", "Colors", "Rafter angles", "Wire"]'::jsonb,2,'Speed squares have pitch scales.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.E',4,'The ''plumb cut'' on a rafter is the cut that sits:','["In the foundation", "Horizontal on the floor", "Under the sill", "Vertical at the ridge"]'::jsonb,3,'Plumb cut meets the ridge board.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.F',3,'Roof sheathing is typically:','["Plywood or OSB panels", "Foam only", "Glass", "Carpet"]'::jsonb,0,'Panels cover rafters.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.F',4,'Plywood clips (H-clips) between roof panels are used to:','["Hide nails", "Support the panel edges", "Cut panels", "Hold paint"]'::jsonb,1,'Clips prevent edge sag.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.G',3,'Gable end vents are installed to:','["Block air", "Hold paint", "Let attic air circulate", "Add weight"]'::jsonb,2,'Ventilation reduces moisture.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.G',4,'Without attic ventilation, hot summer air can:','["Cool shingles", "Reduce moisture", "Lower bills", "Damage shingles and raise cooling costs"]'::jsonb,3,'Ventilation protects the roof.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.H',3,'When setting trusses, the first truss is braced:','["To stay plumb and in position", "Upside down", "Loosely", "Without nails"]'::jsonb,0,'Bracing prevents collapse.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.H',4,'Trusses are usually spaced:','["Randomly", "24 inches on center", "8 feet", "1 inch"]'::jsonb,1,'Common spacing for trusses.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.I',3,'A roof opening for a chimney is framed with:','["Sod", "Paper", "Headers and trimmers", "Shingles"]'::jsonb,2,'Headers frame openings.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.I',4,'Framing around a roof opening must keep:','["Zero clearance", "Nothing", "The wood touching bricks", "Clearance from the chimney as required by code"]'::jsonb,3,'Codes require clearances.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.J',3,'To estimate roof sheathing, you first find the:','["Total roof area", "Window count", "Wall paint", "Sky color"]'::jsonb,0,'Area drives panel count.','original'),
('127.98.d.24.J',4,'A roof 20 feet by 30 feet in plan with a pitch factor of 1.12 has about how much surface area?','["600 square feet", "672 square feet", "540 square feet", "720 square feet"]'::jsonb,1,'600 x 1.12 = 672.','original'),
('127.98.d.25.A',3,'A window that opens by sliding sideways is a:','["Awning", "Fixed", "Slider", "Casement"]'::jsonb,2,'Sliders move horizontally.','original'),
('127.98.d.25.A',4,'A window that is hinged on the side and cranks open is a:','["Double-hung", "Fixed window", "Hopper", "Casement window"]'::jsonb,3,'Casements swing outward.','original'),
('127.98.d.25.B',3,'The horizontal piece at the bottom of a window opening is the:','["Sill", "Head", "Mullion", "Jamb"]'::jsonb,0,'Sill sheds water.','original'),
('127.98.d.25.B',4,'The flashing around a window prevents:','["Glare", "Water leaks into the wall", "Dust", "Noise"]'::jsonb,1,'Flashing diverts water.','original'),
('127.98.d.25.C',3,'Proper window installation requires the unit to be:','["Crooked", "Unsealed", "Plumb, level, square, and flashed", "Wet"]'::jsonb,2,'Correct install prevents leaks.','original'),
('127.98.d.25.C',4,'Shims placed at hinge locations on a window help to:','["Change color", "Hide holes", "Add weight", "Keep the frame from bowing"]'::jsonb,3,'Shims support the frame.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
