('127.98.d.25.G',3,'A threshold at an exterior door helps to:','["Hold paint", "Block air only", "Seal the bottom of the opening", "Add height"]'::jsonb,2,'Thresholds reduce drafts.','original'),
('127.98.d.25.G',4,'An adjustable threshold lets installers:','["Change the door color", "Make noise", "Lengthen the door", "Fine-tune the seal against the door bottom"]'::jsonb,3,'Adjustment improves sealing.','original'),
('127.98.d.25.H',3,'Before installing a pre-hung exterior door, the carpenter should check that the sill is:','["Level", "Wet", "Bare", "Rough"]'::jsonb,0,'A level sill ensures square fit.','original'),
('127.98.d.25.H',4,'The door is secured to the framing through the jamb with:','["Tape", "Screws and shims", "Paper", "Staples"]'::jsonb,1,'Proper fastening keeps alignment.','original'),
('127.98.d.25.I',3,'A lockset that turns a bolt to secure the door is a:','["Passage latch", "Hinge", "Deadbolt", "Door stop"]'::jsonb,2,'Deadbolts add security.','original'),
('127.98.d.25.I',4,'The part of a lock that extends into the doorframe is the:','["Escutcheon", "Knob", "Strike", "Bolt"]'::jsonb,3,'The bolt engages the strike.','original'),
('127.98.d.25.J',3,'Installing a lockset begins with:','["Drilling holes to the template", "Cutting the jamb", "Guessing", "Painting"]'::jsonb,0,'Templates guide drilling.','original'),
('127.98.d.25.J',4,'The strike plate is installed on the:','["Door face", "Door jamb", "Hinge", "Threshold"]'::jsonb,1,'The strike receives the bolt.','original'),
('127.98.d.25.K',3,'A storm door is installed to:','["Lower security", "Remove the main door", "Add protection and insulation to the entry", "Block daylight"]'::jsonb,2,'Storm doors provide protection.','original'),
('127.98.d.25.K',4,'A garage overhead door is moved by:','["Magnets only", "Rope only", "Ice", "Springs and tracks"]'::jsonb,3,'Springs counterbalance weight.','original'),
('127.98.d.26.A',3,'A staircase with a landing and a turn is a:','["L-shaped or U-shaped stair", "Ladder", "Straight run only", "Spiral only"]'::jsonb,0,'Landings allow turns.','original'),
('127.98.d.26.A',4,'A stair that winds around a central post is a:','["Winder", "Spiral stair", "Box stair", "Straight stair"]'::jsonb,1,'Spiral stairs save space.','original'),
('127.98.d.26.B',3,'The vertical part of a step is the:','["Tread", "Stringer", "Riser", "Nosing"]'::jsonb,2,'The riser is the rise.','original'),
('127.98.d.26.B',4,'The horizontal part of a step is the:','["Riser", "Baluster", "Newel", "Tread"]'::jsonb,3,'You step on the tread.','original'),
('127.98.d.26.C',3,'Stringers are the angled supports that carry the:','["Treads and risers", "Windows", "Roof", "Doors"]'::jsonb,0,'Stringers hold steps.','original'),
('127.98.d.26.C',4,'Exterior stairs should use:','["Paper", "Pressure-treated or weather-resistant materials", "Cardboard", "Foam"]'::jsonb,1,'Weather exposure demands durable lumber.','original'),
('127.98.d.26.D',3,'On a stair drawing, the number of risers is usually:','["Hidden", "Painted", "Marked with an arrow and a count", "Left out"]'::jsonb,2,'Drawings show the count.','original'),
('127.98.d.26.D',4,'A stair detail shows dimensions for the:','["Light switch", "Door color", "Paint color", "Rise, run, and headroom"]'::jsonb,3,'Stair plans list critical numbers.','original'),
('127.98.d.26.E',3,'A total rise of 108 inches divided by a 7.5-inch riser gives about how many risers?','["14", "8", "10", "18"]'::jsonb,0,'108 / 7.5 = 14.4, so use 14.','original'),
('127.98.d.26.E',4,'If a stair has 14 risers and each tread is 10 inches, the total run (13 treads) is:','["140 inches", "130 inches", "100 inches", "108 inches"]'::jsonb,1,'13 x 10 = 130.','original'),
('127.98.d.29.A',3,'A team is a group of people who:','["Work alone", "Argue constantly", "Work together toward a common goal", "Compete only"]'::jsonb,2,'Teams share purpose.','original'),
('127.98.d.29.A',4,'A framing crew working in sequence shows that teams function best when members:','["Block each other", "Work in secret", "Ignore others", "Communicate and depend on each other"]'::jsonb,3,'Coordination drives success.','original'),
('127.98.d.29.B',3,'Using teamwork to solve a problem means:','["Sharing ideas and combining strengths", "Hiding ideas", "Working alone", "Ignoring input"]'::jsonb,0,'Many minds solve problems.','original'),
('127.98.d.29.B',4,'If a roof cut doesn''t fit, a team solves it best by:','["Walking away", "Discussing options and testing a fix together", "Ignoring it", "Blaming one worker"]'::jsonb,1,'Collaboration leads to solutions.','original'),
('127.98.d.29.C',3,'A team leader typically:','["Does nothing", "Quits", "Guides the team and keeps work on track", "Sleeps"]'::jsonb,2,'Leaders organize.','original'),
('127.98.d.29.C',4,'A team member''s role is to:','["Compete with leader", "Ignore tasks", "Skip meetings", "Complete assigned tasks and support the team"]'::jsonb,3,'Members contribute.','original'),
('127.98.d.29.D',4,'Leaders earn trust by:','["Keeping promises and setting an example", "Avoiding responsibility", "Blaming others", "Breaking promises"]'::jsonb,0,'Integrity matters.','original'),
('127.98.d.29.E',3,'Employers expect workers to arrive on time and:','["Skip training", "Complete tasks reliably", "Ignore safety", "Take long breaks"]'::jsonb,1,'Reliability is valued.','original'),
('127.98.d.29.E',4,'A good work habit in a shop is to:','["Leave tools out", "Drop trash", "Clean up your area at the end of the shift", "Skip cleanup"]'::jsonb,2,'Cleanup is part of the job.','original'),
('127.98.d.29.F',3,'Harassment in the workplace is:','["Friendly teamwork", "Normal feedback", "A safety rule", "Unwelcome behavior that creates a hostile environment"]'::jsonb,3,'Harassment is prohibited.','original'),
('127.98.d.29.F',4,'Discrimination means:','["Treating people unfairly because of a protected trait", "Giving everyone the same tools", "Following a schedule", "Hiring the most skilled"]'::jsonb,0,'It is illegal.','original'),
('127.98.d.29.G',3,'A time-management technique is to:','["Ignore due dates", "Make a daily task list and prioritize", "Wait until deadlines", "Work without plan"]'::jsonb,1,'Lists organize time.','original'),
('127.98.d.29.G',4,'Doing the most important task first, before minor ones, is called:','["Procrastinating", "Skipping", "Prioritizing", "Daydreaming"]'::jsonb,2,'Prioritizing maximizes results.','original'),
('127.103.d.1.A',3,'A concrete masonry unit (CMU) is commonly called a:','["Clay brick", "Wood plank", "Glass tile", "Concrete block"]'::jsonb,3,'CMUs are made of concrete.','original'),
('127.103.d.1.A',4,'Hollow CMUs are used in walls because they are:','["Strong, lighter than solid units, and able to be filled with grout and rebar", "Soft and weak", "Waterproof by nature", "Made of wood"]'::jsonb,0,'Cores allow reinforcement.','original'),
('127.103.d.1.B',3,'Bricks are made from:','["Plastic", "Clay fired in a kiln", "Wood chips", "Paper"]'::jsonb,1,'Fired clay hardens into brick.','original'),
('127.103.d.1.B',4,'Brick veneer on a house is mostly:','["The main load-bearing frame", "The roof", "A decorative, weather-resistant outer layer", "The foundation"]'::jsonb,2,'Veneer is attached to the structure.','original'),
('127.103.d.1.C',3,'Natural stone used in construction includes:','["Steel", "Plastic", "Cardboard", "Granite and limestone"]'::jsonb,3,'Stone is quarried.','original'),
('127.103.d.1.C',4,'Stone is valued as a building material for its:','["Durability and appearance", "Flexibility", "Low weight", "Transparency"]'::jsonb,0,'Stone lasts for centuries.','original'),
('127.103.d.1.D',3,'Mortar is used to:','["Cut bricks", "Bond masonry units together", "Level the ground", "Paint walls"]'::jsonb,1,'Mortar is the glue of masonry.','original'),
('127.103.d.1.D',4,'Grout differs from mortar because it is:','["Sand only", "A tool", "Thinner and poured into cores or cavities", "Dry powder"]'::jsonb,2,'Grout fills voids.','original'),
('127.103.d.1.E',3,'In a running bond, each course of masonry units is:','["Rotated 90 degrees", "Left empty", "Stacked straight above", "Offset from the one below by half a unit"]'::jsonb,3,'Staggering distributes loads.','original'),
('127.103.d.1.E',4,'A wall built with one unit thickness is called a:','["Single-wythe wall", "Cavity foundation", "Pier-only", "Double-roof"]'::jsonb,0,'A wythe is a single vertical layer.','original'),
('127.103.d.2.A',3,'Besides medical bills, an accident on a masonry site can cost a company:','["More profit", "Lost production and higher insurance", "Faster work", "Free equipment"]'::jsonb,1,'Direct and indirect costs add up.','original'),
('127.103.d.2.A',4,'Fewer jobsite accidents help a mason''s employer to:','["Lose workers", "Raise accident rates", "Lower insurance premiums", "Pay more fines"]'::jsonb,2,'Safety saves money.','original'),
('127.103.d.2.B',3,'A common cause of accidents on a masonry job is:','["Wearing PPE", "Planning", "Training", "Unsafe acts like rushing and skipping PPE"]'::jsonb,3,'Human behavior causes most accidents.','original'),
('127.103.d.2.B',4,'Poor lighting and cluttered scaffolds are unsafe:','["Conditions", "Holidays", "Habits", "Rewards"]'::jsonb,0,'Conditions contribute to accidents.','original'),
('127.103.d.2.C',3,'A masonry worker should recognize hazards such as:','["Colorful paint", "Falling bricks and blocks", "Clean walkways", "Quiet tools"]'::jsonb,1,'Falling objects injure.','original'),
('127.103.d.2.C',4,'Silica dust from cutting block is a hazard because it can damage the:','["Hair", "Knees", "Lungs", "Eyes only"]'::jsonb,2,'Silica causes serious lung disease.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
